answer:
redirect() works, but Reflex event handlers trigger frontend actions by returning events, not by calling them. rx.redirect("/nextpage") only builds an event spec; if you don't return it, it's discarded — which is why your print fires but the page never changes. Fix:

class State(rx.State):
    def next(self):
        print("Going to next")
        return rx.redirect("/nextpage")

Anything a handler wants the browser to do — rx.redirect, rx.toast, rx.download, rx.set_value, etc. — must be returned, or yielded if you want to emit multiple events or stream updates:

    def next(self):
        yield rx.redirect("/nextpage")

With the return (or yield) in place, clicking the button navigates to /nextpage. Your page registration (app.add_page(nextpage, route="/nextpage")) is already correct.
